and Theorem 1.3. Finally, in Section 4, we establish the relationship between the M-triangle and H-triangle, proving Corollary 1.4.\n\n\n## Section snippets\n\n### Preliminaries\n\nFor integers , consider the two disjoint sets of letters\n\nDefinition 2.1\n\nA is a word over in which no letter appears more than once such that, whenever , if {x}_{i} and {x}_{j} both appear in the word, then {x}_{i} appears before {x}_{j} and if {y}_{i} and {y}_{j} both appear in the word, then {y}_{i} appears before {y}_{j}. Let be the set of all shuffle words.\n\nFor example, the word belongs to , but the words and do not. Throughout this paper, we use bold\n\n### The M-triangle of\n\nThe M-triangle is defined by McConville and Mühle as the bivariate generalization of the reverse characteristic polynomial, providing us more combinatorial information than its single-variable analog. We restate the definition of the M-triangle, which we also provided in the introduction:\n\nDefinition 1.1\n\nThe of a graded poset P is\n\nIn this paper, we are interested in the M-triangle of . So, let denote , let denote\n\n### Relating the M-triangle and the H-triangle\n\nFinally, we offer a surprising connection between the M-triangle and the H-triangle, a refinement of the f-polynomial of an auxilary simplicial complex associated with a related lattice, the .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nG. Hurlbert Publications (https://glennhurlbert.github.io/pubs.html)\nciteturn9search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago;  | 12    | Equivalence class universal cycles for permutations (with G. Isaak), Discrete Math. 149 (1996), no.1-3, 123--129.\n | 12    | Equivalence class universal cycles for permutations (with G. Isaak), Discrete Math. 149 (1996), no.1-3, 123--129.\n | 11    | New constructions for De Bruijn tori (with G. Isaak), Des. Codes Cryptogr. 6 (1995), no.1, 47--56.\n | 10    | Multicover ucycles, Discrete Math. 137 (1995), no.1-3, 241--249.\n 